WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.892
This is the technical deep dive for
Security Operations and Telemetry,

3
00:00:04.892 --> 00:00:08.112
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:11.191
It's for the people who
build and run the SOC:

5
00:00:11.191 --> 00:00:15.675
detection engineers, SOC
engineers and security architects.

6
00:00:15.675 --> 00:00:18.037
The whole book comes down to one idea.

7
00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:20.188
You can't defend what you can't see,

8
00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:24.726
and a finding only closes when the
thing is actually fixed, with proof.

9
00:00:24.726 --> 00:00:29.425
Part 1 is about getting evidence to
arrive and turning it into detections.

10
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:33.188
Part 2 is about the findings that
pile up, and who acts on them.

11
00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:36.726
The code lives in the chapters;
I'll stick to what matters.

12
00:00:36.726 --> 00:00:39.560
Let's start with why this
is harder than it looks.

13
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.085
Here's the uncomfortable part.

14
00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:47.748
Every dashboard you own
measures what arrived.

15
00:00:47.748 --> 00:00:49.259
Nothing measures what didn't.

16
00:00:49.259 --> 00:00:51.320
A source that was never connected,

17
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.102
a connector that looks green but isn't
delivering, data parked where no rule can

18
00:00:56.102 --> 00:01:00.810
run, or logging an attacker switched off:
all four look exactly like a quiet day.

19
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.260
And the federal yardstick
has moved with this.

20
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.610
The new logging memo scores
agencies on how much of their

21
00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.960
baseline produces real alerts,
not on how much they store.

22
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.834
So visibility has to be
designed, plane by plane,

23
00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:15.560
and checked against what
you expected to see.

24
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.810
That's what Module 1 builds.

25
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.672
Module 1 is the foundation.

26
00:01:23.672 --> 00:01:26.713
First, one record shape
that every tool agrees to,

27
00:01:26.713 --> 00:01:30.361
so findings from different
stacks can actually be joined.

28
00:01:30.361 --> 00:01:32.101
Then we apply it to identity,

29
00:01:32.101 --> 00:01:36.230
where the default quietly throws
history away after a month.

30
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.193
Five tools can't give you one compliance
picture just by sharing a dashboard.

31
00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:48.068
They have to agree, ahead of
time, on what a record says.

32
00:01:48.068 --> 00:01:51.976
That's the evidence contract:
eight fields every source emits,

33
00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:55.884
covering what NIST already asks
an audit record to establish,

34
00:01:55.884 --> 00:02:00.329
plus which control it proves, whether
it passed, and whether it's closed.

35
00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.093
That last field matters most,
because it's where closure lives.

36
00:02:04.093 --> 00:02:07.457
Microsoft's normalization
model gets you part of the way,

37
00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:10.606
but it can't tell you which
real asset a record is about.

38
00:02:10.606 --> 00:02:14.050
And that turns out to be the
hard part, so let's look at it.

39
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.844
Two records only describe
the same server if they

40
00:02:20.844 --> 00:02:23.888
carry the same identity,
and every tool has its own.

41
00:02:23.888 --> 00:02:26.700
So the join key can't
come from any one tool.

42
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.950
It comes from the naming plane, your
authoritative inventory of names and addresses.

43
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.674
Time is the other half, and it's
easier to get wrong than it sounds:

44
00:02:35.674 --> 00:02:39.040
join on when data arrived
instead of when it was observed,

45
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.262
and you match the wrong
records without a single error.

46
00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:47.295
One caution worth knowing: the fully
correlated trail is a High-baseline control,

47
00:02:47.295 --> 00:02:50.299
so at Moderate the program
chooses it on purpose.

48
00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:52.900
Next, where all these records go.

49
00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.866
A missing patch, a drifted setting,

50
00:02:58.866 --> 00:03:04.168
an exposed storage account and a labeled
file leaving the agency are all the same

51
00:03:04.168 --> 00:03:09.075
kind of statement as a threat alert: this
asset isn't in the state it should be.

52
00:03:09.075 --> 00:03:10.712
So they all go in one queue.

53
00:03:10.712 --> 00:03:11.925
Two lessons here.

54
00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:15.923
Queue membership can change underneath
you; a vendor default recently

55
00:03:15.923 --> 00:03:20.125
moved data loss alerts out of the
incident queue without anything failing.

56
00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:24.549
And only two things close a record:
a later observation showing the fix,

57
00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:27.600
or the authorizing official
accepting the risk.

58
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.160
Now, identity.

59
00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.185
Identity looks instrumented out of
the box, which is exactly the trap.

60
00:03:37.185 --> 00:03:41.218
Entra keeps its own sign-in and
audit history for about a month,

61
00:03:41.218 --> 00:03:43.985
and once it's gone, no
license brings it back.

62
00:03:43.985 --> 00:03:47.735
The fix is one tenant-level
setting that streams the logs out.

63
00:03:47.735 --> 00:03:50.658
The detail that bites
people is that the category

64
00:03:50.658 --> 00:03:53.010
you tick isn't always the table you query.

65
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.470
The big decision is to treat all
four sign-in streams as core,

66
00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:01.272
because the non-interactive ones are where
unattended credentials do their work.

67
00:04:01.272 --> 00:04:05.473
Manage the cost with table plans,
never by switching a stream off.

68
00:04:05.473 --> 00:04:08.100
Next, how to deploy it properly.

69
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:16.762
A setting someone clicked once has no
history, no review and no drift check.

70
00:04:16.762 --> 00:04:19.322
So the program deploys this one from code,

71
00:04:19.322 --> 00:04:23.763
and keeps its list of logged categories
in the repository as the record.

72
00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:24.825
Then retention.

73
00:04:24.825 --> 00:04:27.383
Here's a trap that catches
a lot of templates:

74
00:04:27.383 --> 00:04:31.788
the retention field on a diagnostic
setting no longer does anything at all.

75
00:04:31.788 --> 00:04:35.762
Retention is set per table in the
workspace, at least six months

76
00:04:35.762 --> 00:04:39.813
searchable and a year retrievable,
in line with the logging memo.

77
00:04:39.813 --> 00:04:43.937
Of course, a setting that saved
isn't proof anything is arriving.

78
00:04:43.937 --> 00:04:45.340
That's next.

79
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.750
Saving a setting proves nothing.

80
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:54.794
So the program runs a completeness
check a day after any change,

81
00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:59.850
again at three days, and then daily, against
a named list of the tables it expects.

82
00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.375
The result to fear is stale: it worked,
then stopped, and old data hides the gap.

83
00:05:05.375 --> 00:05:07.592
Microsoft Graph is useful here,

84
00:05:07.592 --> 00:05:12.112
but as a magnifying glass for one
specific question, not as a feed.

85
00:05:12.112 --> 00:05:15.411
And watch one silent trap:
without the right permission,

86
00:05:15.411 --> 00:05:19.212
sign-in records quietly drop
their Conditional Access results.

87
00:05:19.212 --> 00:05:20.325
That's identity.

88
00:05:20.325 --> 00:05:23.950
Module 2 moves to the
platform and the mailbox.

89
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:32.817
Module 2 covers the two planes the
identity setting never touches,

90
00:05:32.817 --> 00:05:36.538
Azure resources and the
Microsoft 365 audit log.

91
00:05:36.538 --> 00:05:38.412
Then it asks the real question:

92
00:05:38.412 --> 00:05:42.830
what turns all this stored data
into detections someone can show?

93
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.955
Azure has two planes, and
they behave very differently.

94
00:05:49.955 --> 00:05:54.592
The activity log is on by default and
records who created and changed things,

95
00:05:54.592 --> 00:05:57.905
but it's deleted after ninety
days unless you export it.

96
00:05:57.905 --> 00:06:02.091
Resource logs, what actually happened
inside a key vault or a database,

97
00:06:02.091 --> 00:06:05.917
aren't collected at all until each
resource gets its own setting.

98
00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:10.305
The engineering choice that pays off
later is sending them to dedicated tables.

99
00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:13.695
The shared table runs out of
columns, and when it does,

100
00:06:13.695 --> 00:06:16.455
a detection rule silently stops matching.

101
00:06:16.455 --> 00:06:19.130
So how do you keep every
new resource emitting?

102
00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.330
Policy.

103
00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.657
Azure Policy can make logging
a property of the platform,

104
00:06:27.657 --> 00:06:30.093
so new resources are never born silent.

105
00:06:30.093 --> 00:06:31.917
But here's what catches teams out.

106
00:06:31.917 --> 00:06:35.626
The policy fixes new and
changed resources automatically,

107
00:06:35.626 --> 00:06:40.243
and for everything that already existed,
it only marks them non-compliant.

108
00:06:40.243 --> 00:06:43.930
Nothing gets deployed until
someone runs a remediation task.

109
00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.018
So the dashboard trends green
while the old estate stays dark.

110
00:06:48.018 --> 00:06:51.737
The program's habit is simple:
assign, run the remediation,

111
00:06:51.737 --> 00:06:55.456
then compare the compliant
count against the inventory.

112
00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:58.130
That comparison is the
real coverage number.

113
00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:02.200
Next, the Microsoft 365 audit log.

114
00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:12.213
The Microsoft 365 audit log is the plane
most often assumed and least often checked.

115
00:07:12.213 --> 00:07:16.345
Verify it's actually on, and do it
in the right PowerShell session,

116
00:07:16.345 --> 00:07:18.762
because the other one always says off.

117
00:07:18.762 --> 00:07:23.889
Collecting it through Microsoft's API has
a few rules that break almost every first

118
00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:28.346
attempt: patience when you subscribe,
a seven-day window to fetch content,

119
00:07:28.346 --> 00:07:31.838
events that arrive out of order,
and duplicates by design.

120
00:07:31.838 --> 00:07:35.254
So the collector tracks what
it's fetched, deduplicates,

121
00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:38.150
and looks back over a
trailing window every run.

122
00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.740
Then there's how long Microsoft keeps it.

123
00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:46.065
This one surprises people.

124
00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:50.212
How long a Microsoft 365 audit
record survives depends on the

125
00:07:50.212 --> 00:07:53.953
license of the person who did
the thing, not on the tenant.

126
00:07:53.953 --> 00:07:57.795
Fully licensed users get a
year for the core workloads;

127
00:07:57.795 --> 00:08:01.552
everyone else, including
every guest, gets six months.

128
00:08:01.552 --> 00:08:03.528
Most federal tenants are mixed,

129
00:08:03.528 --> 00:08:07.478
so they have a split retention
horizon that no dashboard shows.

130
00:08:07.478 --> 00:08:11.603
The program's answer is to stop treating
that tier as the evidence store.

131
00:08:11.603 --> 00:08:15.498
Collect everything into your own
workspace, set retention there,

132
00:08:15.498 --> 00:08:18.528
and write the license split
into the security plan.

133
00:08:18.528 --> 00:08:20.840
Now, what do we do with all this data?

134
00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.090
Here's the heart of the book.

135
00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:30.452
A place where logs are kept isn't the
same as a capability that reads them.

136
00:08:30.452 --> 00:08:34.927
A workspace with no rules can prove
storage; it can't prove analysis.

137
00:08:34.927 --> 00:08:38.297
So the program's rule is that
no source counts as monitored

138
00:08:38.297 --> 00:08:41.877
until an enabled rule reads it
and someone owns what it raises.

139
00:08:41.877 --> 00:08:46.427
The rule types also set a floor on how
fast you can possibly detect anything.

140
00:08:46.427 --> 00:08:49.278
And get ready for the
assessor's favorite request:

141
00:08:49.278 --> 00:08:52.477
show me an alert from last month,
and what you did about it.

142
00:08:52.477 --> 00:08:54.910
Next, how the data gets in.

143
00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.187
Every source reaches Sentinel by its
own path and lands in its own table,

144
00:09:03.187 --> 00:09:05.873
and each path has one
gotcha worth knowing.

145
00:09:05.873 --> 00:09:09.899
Appliances like DNS and SASE
come through a forwarder that

146
00:09:09.899 --> 00:09:13.523
stamps its own time, which
matters when you join on time.

147
00:09:13.523 --> 00:09:18.772
Write DNS rules against the normalized
parser, so they survive a vendor change.

148
00:09:18.772 --> 00:09:20.760
And plan in the Defender portal,

149
00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.423
because that's the only place Sentinel
will be supported after March 2027.

150
00:09:25.423 --> 00:09:26.846
In the government clouds,

151
00:09:26.846 --> 00:09:30.372
check each connector in your own
portal before you design on it.

152
00:09:30.372 --> 00:09:32.940
But how do you know a
connector's still working?

153
00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.447
A connector that silently stops is
worse than one you never set up,

154
00:09:40.447 --> 00:09:42.465
because you believe you're covered.

155
00:09:42.465 --> 00:09:45.604
Microsoft's health table
only covers some connectors,

156
00:09:45.604 --> 00:09:47.766
so the program checks the data itself:

157
00:09:47.766 --> 00:09:52.090
when did each table last hear something,
and is that longer than it should be?

158
00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:55.740
Make that check a rule, so a
quiet source opens an incident.

159
00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.365
Then divide the work with Defender XDR.

160
00:09:58.365 --> 00:10:01.128
Let Defender detect what
its own sensors see,

161
00:10:01.128 --> 00:10:04.115
and point Sentinel at
everything Defender can't.

162
00:10:04.115 --> 00:10:05.115
That's Part 1.

163
00:10:05.115 --> 00:10:07.710
Part 2 starts with vulnerabilities.

164
00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:14.685
Module 3 is about findings and the clock.

165
00:10:14.685 --> 00:10:18.548
A vulnerability program
is judged on one record:

166
00:10:18.548 --> 00:10:22.798
which flaw, which asset, found
when, due when, fixed when.

167
00:10:22.798 --> 00:10:27.155
We'll see the new federal clock, hand the
work to the platforms that can fix it,

168
00:10:27.155 --> 00:10:29.690
and finish in the cloud control plane.

169
00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.940
In June 2026, CISA replaced the old
severity buckets with a smarter clock.

170
00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:42.644
Four questions about each
vulnerable asset set the deadline:

171
00:10:42.644 --> 00:10:46.057
is it exposed to the internet,
is the flaw being exploited,

172
00:10:46.057 --> 00:10:50.415
can the attack be automated, and how
much control does it give an attacker.

173
00:10:50.415 --> 00:10:52.597
The answers range from three days,

174
00:10:52.597 --> 00:10:56.103
plus a forensic check, down to
fix it at the next upgrade.

175
00:10:56.103 --> 00:10:57.602
Two points to hold onto.

176
00:10:57.602 --> 00:11:01.452
The clock starts at the earliest
moment, and there are no waivers.

177
00:11:01.452 --> 00:11:06.040
By December, every externally
reachable asset needs an exposure tag.

178
00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.350
So, how do you run that daily?

179
00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.817
The daily job joins four things: what
the scanner found, CISA's list of

180
00:11:16.817 --> 00:11:22.362
exploited flaws, CISA's data on automation
and impact, and your own exposure tags.

181
00:11:22.362 --> 00:11:24.750
Two design choices make it trustworthy.

182
00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:28.862
When data is missing, the program
assumes the stricter answer.

183
00:11:28.862 --> 00:11:31.957
And it remembers when each
finding was first seen,

184
00:11:31.957 --> 00:11:36.412
because recalculating that every
morning quietly resets every clock.

185
00:11:36.412 --> 00:11:40.458
Report how many findings closed on
time and how many are past due,

186
00:11:40.458 --> 00:11:45.725
never a percentage of the open queue, which
improves every time new findings arrive.

187
00:11:45.725 --> 00:11:48.780
Next, proving the scanner sees everything.

188
00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:57.243
The scanner choice comes down to one
property: the sensor follows the inventory.

189
00:11:57.243 --> 00:12:01.980
Enroll a server in Azure Arc, in a
covered subscription, and it's scanned.

190
00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:03.968
But enrollment isn't coverage.

191
00:12:03.968 --> 00:12:08.858
An extension can fail, a sensor can go
unhealthy, so the program checks daily

192
00:12:08.858 --> 00:12:14.143
that every enrolled server is actually
reporting, and treats any gap as a finding.

193
00:12:14.143 --> 00:12:17.612
And lifecycle sets a hard
edge: a server whose security

194
00:12:17.612 --> 00:12:21.543
updates have ended can still be
scanned, but nothing can fix it.

195
00:12:21.543 --> 00:12:26.154
That's a decision for the authorizing
official, not a row that ages forever.

196
00:12:26.154 --> 00:12:28.420
Now, who actually does the fixing?

197
00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:35.032
Only the platform that runs
a workload can fix it.

198
00:12:35.032 --> 00:12:38.494
So the program lets each
platform patch with the tools

199
00:12:38.494 --> 00:12:42.108
its support model expects, in
every cloud and on premises.

200
00:12:42.108 --> 00:12:45.684
What's unified is the record
every one of them sends back,

201
00:12:45.684 --> 00:12:47.783
keyed to the same asset identity.

202
00:12:47.783 --> 00:12:52.358
One thing no single tool can reach is
the hypervisor and firmware layer,

203
00:12:52.358 --> 00:12:54.995
so don't let an overlay promise otherwise.

204
00:12:54.995 --> 00:12:58.889
And remember, a finding closes
only when both halves agree:

205
00:12:58.889 --> 00:13:03.757
the platform says what it applied, and
a later scan no longer sees the flaw.

206
00:13:03.757 --> 00:13:05.344
Patching is half the job.

207
00:13:05.344 --> 00:13:07.560
Configuration is the other.

208
00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:15.448
A patch that drifts back out of its
hardened settings hasn't stayed fixed.

209
00:13:15.448 --> 00:13:17.670
So baselines are written as code,

210
00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:21.948
rolled out in audit mode first,
and every drift becomes a record.

211
00:13:21.948 --> 00:13:23.861
There's one setting worth knowing well.

212
00:13:23.861 --> 00:13:26.854
Azure's auto-correct mode
fixes drift silently,

213
00:13:26.854 --> 00:13:30.847
and Microsoft says it can't report
that the drift ever happened.

214
00:13:30.847 --> 00:13:35.522
That means an attacker's change can
be quietly undone and leave no trace.

215
00:13:35.522 --> 00:13:38.263
So use auto-correct only
for low-risk settings,

216
00:13:38.263 --> 00:13:41.509
and keep visible drift wherever
you need the evidence.

217
00:13:41.509 --> 00:13:45.372
And a server that drops out of the
report isn't clean; it's a gap.

218
00:13:45.372 --> 00:13:47.560
Next, the cloud itself.

219
00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:55.847
A public storage bucket has no missing
patch, so no scanner will ever flag it.

220
00:13:55.847 --> 00:13:59.348
Cloud posture tools read the
cloud's own settings instead.

221
00:13:59.348 --> 00:14:02.236
The program's rule for
choosing them is practical:

222
00:14:02.236 --> 00:14:06.674
where you already own a platform's native
tool, it's primary for that platform,

223
00:14:06.674 --> 00:14:10.760
and a coordinating tool earns its
role only where it actually reaches.

224
00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:14.874
Check every listing against the
FedRAMP Marketplace, not memory,

225
00:14:14.874 --> 00:14:18.835
and know the gaps, like no Oracle
coverage from Microsoft's tool.

226
00:14:18.835 --> 00:14:21.106
For containers, enforce at the gate:

227
00:14:21.106 --> 00:14:25.335
refuse the privileged or unscanned
workload before it ever runs.

228
00:14:25.335 --> 00:14:27.820
Module 4 turns to data.

229
00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.845
Module 4 closes the book
with two questions.

230
00:14:34.845 --> 00:14:36.445
What is actually in the file?

231
00:14:36.445 --> 00:14:40.480
And who decides what the security
team sees, keeps and lets go?

232
00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.774
Network and identity controls
can all pass while a sensitive

233
00:14:47.774 --> 00:14:51.318
file walks out the door, because
nothing said what the file was.

234
00:14:51.318 --> 00:14:55.680
A sensitivity label supplies that
signal, and other tools can read it.

235
00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.340
The federal rules are simpler than
people think: there are only two levels,

236
00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:04.389
Basic and Specified, and the CUI
Registry decides which applies,

237
00:15:04.389 --> 00:15:06.605
not how sensitive something feels.

238
00:15:06.605 --> 00:15:09.630
Privacy Act records,
for example, are Basic,

239
00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.655
while taxpayer information is Specified.

240
00:15:12.655 --> 00:15:15.112
The program maps those levels to labels,

241
00:15:15.112 --> 00:15:19.505
encrypts to groups rather than people,
and deploys the whole set as code.

242
00:15:19.505 --> 00:15:21.900
Then the labels have to be enforced.

243
00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:28.038
Labels only matter if
something acts on them.

244
00:15:28.038 --> 00:15:31.262
Data loss prevention acts in three places:

245
00:15:31.262 --> 00:15:36.788
inside the Microsoft 365 services, on
the device, and at the cloud session.

246
00:15:36.788 --> 00:15:40.063
The program's minimum set of
policies is on the slide.

247
00:15:40.063 --> 00:15:42.538
The rollout discipline
is the part to copy.

248
00:15:42.538 --> 00:15:47.007
Every policy starts in simulation,
measured against real content,

249
00:15:47.007 --> 00:15:50.338
because a false-positive
storm that stamps CUI on

250
00:15:50.338 --> 00:15:53.913
routine files becomes an
agency-wide help desk event.

251
00:15:53.913 --> 00:15:57.862
And once enforcement starts, watch
the people who keep overriding it.

252
00:15:57.862 --> 00:16:01.180
Next, the encryption keys behind all this.

253
00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:08.310
When someone says bring your
own key, ask which one,

254
00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:12.268
because in Microsoft 365 it
means three different things.

255
00:16:12.268 --> 00:16:15.393
One protects every
encrypted label at the root.

256
00:16:15.393 --> 00:16:17.692
One encrypts the services at rest.

257
00:16:17.692 --> 00:16:22.169
And one, double key encryption,
protects only the content you choose,

258
00:16:22.169 --> 00:16:26.567
at the cost of search, eDiscovery
and co-authoring for that content.

259
00:16:26.567 --> 00:16:30.581
The program uses that last one
only for the narrowest categories.

260
00:16:30.581 --> 00:16:35.096
One operational trap: Customer
Key keys must never expire,

261
00:16:35.096 --> 00:16:40.742
so a rotation policy that sets an expiry
date can take Microsoft 365 down.

262
00:16:40.742 --> 00:16:43.840
Which brings us to
people, and who decides.

263
00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:49.735
Most agencies can name their SOC.

264
00:16:49.735 --> 00:16:53.072
Fewer can say who decided
a log source is collected,

265
00:16:53.072 --> 00:16:55.852
or who accepted the risk
when it went quiet.

266
00:16:55.852 --> 00:16:58.302
In practice it's whoever
holds the console.

267
00:16:58.302 --> 00:17:01.429
The book separates three
things: who decides,

268
00:17:01.429 --> 00:17:03.964
who does the work, and who's consulted.

269
00:17:03.964 --> 00:17:06.503
The CIO decides scope and funding.

270
00:17:06.503 --> 00:17:08.627
The CISO sets the requirements.

271
00:17:08.627 --> 00:17:13.477
Only the authorizing official accepts
risk, including the risk of not seeing.

272
00:17:13.477 --> 00:17:17.540
And the SOC runs the queue and
owns the one-hour notice to CISA.

273
00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.260
Let's look at the
decisions that matter most.

274
00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:26.644
These are the decisions
that quietly shrink

275
00:17:26.644 --> 00:17:29.135
what the agency can see,
each with one owner.

276
00:17:29.135 --> 00:17:31.912
The pattern to notice is
that the person who can make

277
00:17:31.912 --> 00:17:34.560
a change is rarely the
person who should decide it.

278
00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:38.635
Lowering retention looks like a
slider, but it's a records decision.

279
00:17:38.635 --> 00:17:42.352
Silencing a noisy rule with an
end date is the CISO's call;

280
00:17:42.352 --> 00:17:47.098
silencing it forever is a risk
acceptance for the authorizing official.

281
00:17:47.098 --> 00:17:51.360
And a playbook that disables accounts
needs approval before it ever runs.

282
00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:55.530
Next, how roles and separations
keep the evidence honest.

283
00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:04.921
Every rule and playbook gets exactly one
named owner, with a test and a review date,

284
00:18:04.921 --> 00:18:08.542
so a silent table can be
traced to the rules it blinds.

285
00:18:08.542 --> 00:18:11.096
Each team gets the least access it needs,

286
00:18:11.096 --> 00:18:14.192
with powerful roles
activated only when needed.

287
00:18:14.192 --> 00:18:17.346
And because security
operations keeps the record of

288
00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:20.643
what everyone else did, its
own separations matter most.

289
00:18:20.643 --> 00:18:23.130
Nobody quietly shortens the record.

290
00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.080
Platform owners can't
silence their own logs.

291
00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.655
The SOC doesn't assess itself.

292
00:18:28.655 --> 00:18:32.630
And a contractor can prepare a risk
acceptance, but never sign it.

293
00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.830
Let's close with the decisions.

294
00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.205
So here are the decisions,
each with an owner.

295
00:18:41.205 --> 00:18:44.343
Name what must emit and check
it against what arrives.

296
00:18:44.343 --> 00:18:47.204
Build the naming plane before
the evidence pipeline.

297
00:18:47.204 --> 00:18:50.093
Tag every exposed asset before December.

298
00:18:50.093 --> 00:18:53.692
Decide your key strategy before
labels encrypt anything.

299
00:18:53.692 --> 00:18:58.280
And send every blind spot to the
authorizing official with an end date.

300
00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.165
The good news is that the
first steps cost nothing:

301
00:19:01.165 --> 00:19:05.317
run the checks from this session on
Monday, and put an owner on every rule.

302
00:19:05.317 --> 00:19:07.534
You can't defend what you can't see,

303
00:19:07.534 --> 00:19:10.980
and a finding closes only
when it's fixed, with proof.

304
00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:11.790
Thank you.
